s  and given what we saw in dusseldorf the  other week  you can see why we felt that and  so that was the principal thing on the  friday afternoon  the nhs digital service sent out their  first alert that something was wrong  at 1 pm after receiving reports from  four  nhs trusts of ransomware attacks hitting  multiple nhs services  the number of trusts reporting problems  quadrupled over the next three hours  that was when nhs england declared a  major incident  i arrived back at the office after the  train i know we've got to brief the home  secretary at seven o'clock or whatever  it is you just have to make sure and  that forms a forcing function about  getting the information picture working  out what the comms options are  you know etc etc paul chitchester is the  director of operations  at the ncsc who we met in the previous  episode  as the day went on you started to get a  sense of just the scale and the enormity  of it as obviously people were reporting  in and  and hospital uh you know were being  disrupted  partly that was actually organizations  shutting their systems down as a  precautionary measure  not the wannacry ransomware sort of  having an impact so that was you know a  lot of disruption was done just by sort  of you know trying to sort of uh turn  things off and put them in a safe space  or safe state so that was kind of  through the friday uh we  uh at that point it was really trying to  get our hands on the actual malware and  get our hands on the tactical details  trying to understand what was going on  this was not just a uk  problem the wannacry virus was freezing  screens and demanding ransoms around the  world  with more than 200 000 computers  affected  in at least 150 countries  it's a ransomware worm which means it's  able to spread from  computer to computer without the need  for someone to click  on an infected link victims ranged from  universities in china  to the global delivery firm fedex and  the nissan car plant in sunderland  