  investigate what was going on like what  were you seeing  what was happening so i i know there's a  lot of  outbound connections to um to the smb  service  which was strange for this kind of  malware and uh  what does that mean sorry oh okay yeah  so it was a  it was connecting to other networks like  uh the um  it was trying to remotely connect to  just random networks and it was going  through a list it wasn't  any kind of structure it was just kind  of picking a random ip address  connecting to it  and uh i kind of this is this was not  common for this kind of malware so when  i was digging into it i kind of realized  oh this is this is spreading computers a  computer this isn't  distributed by email or spam uh this is  actually spreading between computers and  so then what happened  um so then i also noticed it was  connecting out to domain which was not  registered  and uh this is something we often do for  our job is if we see  unregistered domains or any kind of uh  anything in the malwa that would allow  us to track the spread we will kind of  get in on that  so um i went and i registered this  domain so that i could see how many  computers were being infected how fast  it was spreading kind of what what  countries it's targeting  and um kind of found out a bit after  that that  the uh activation of the domain also was  uh  capable of killing the malware itself  and uh completely stopping the spread  for those who like me aren't  particularly technical  what this means is that marcus  inadvertently  found a kill switch for the ransomware  worm by registering the name of the  domain he mentioned  in order to have a better sense of the  scale of the attack marcus actually  managed to stop the virus from being  able to spread any further  and how did that feel when you realized  that um  it kind of it didn't feel real because  uh often like malware like this is  incredibly hard to stop and  it was uh it was not this kind of big  feat it was  just like just registering the domain  which we do like  on a daily basis hundreds of times was  enough to disable this threat so it's a  mix of  like wow we've we've stopped something  really big and also  that was way too easy how quickly did  you realize  the enormity of what you'd done um so as  soon as i realized i'd killed it i kind  of i knew how big of  a deal it was because uh obviously the  intention of raising the domain was to  track the spread so we actually knew how  fast it was spreading and how many  systems it was affecting already  so just kind of knowing that we had  stopped it was already a big deal  this all happened that friday afternoon  as the rest of the world of cyber  security  was also still scrambling to understand  the attack  it took time for everyone to realize the  impact of marcus's quick thinking  and even though the rate of new wannacry  infections started to drop  there was still huge numbers of infected  computers to grapple with  it made for a long tense friday night  saturday  and sunday for kieran paul the ncsc  and the top secret cyber team at gchq in  cheltenham  you know it was mayhem because i mean  even in terms of our capabilities at the  time  it was very early on in the ncsc's life  while kieran was an overall charge and  responsible  for briefing ministers in the public  paul was focused on stopping the attack  fixing the damage and finding out who  was responsible  certainly through saturday we we felt  this wasn't a normal  cyber criminal attack and then as we  built up you know over their sort of  following 48 hours we you know we're in  a fortunate position to be able to  pull different parts of that  intelligence jigsaw together  and so we were pretty confident very  early on of of kind of what was going on  there were suspicions took us months to  prove it was suspicions that it was  north korean  because it wore some similarities to  some attacks they've been doing in banks  across the world um in previous months  but because 